        {
          "id": "dc18bc83-3e61-3c2c-97ec-0760b9ca3969",
          "content": {
            "id": "dc18bc83-3e61-3c2c-97ec-0760b9ca3969",
            "contentType": "VIDEO",
            "title": "Audit flags governance gaps at Ben & Jerry\u2019s Foundation",
            "description": "<title><body><p>STORY: The Ben & Jerry\u2019s Foundation, a non-profit funded by the ice cream brand, was found to have deficiencies in financial controls and governance, as well as issues in conflicts of interest.</p><p>That\u2019s according to an audit by Magnum, the Unilever unit set to be spun off next week that will own Ben & Jerry\u2019s.</p><p>Magnum said it conducted an audit as a matter of good governance in preparation for the upcoming spin-off...</p><p>and added that they're trying to work with the foundation by adopting a code of ethics, conflict-of-interest policy, term limits for trustees and due diligence and financial controls on grants.</p><p>The foundation receives about $5 million annually from Ben & Jerry's, and Magnum said it plans to continue fully funding the organization, provided the issues are raised and addressed.</p><p>Ben & Jerry's and the foundation did not respond to requests for comment, but its co-founder Ben Cohen said in October that he expects the conflict between the brand and its new owner to grow after the spin-off.</p><p>Unilever and Magnum have been upping the pressure on Ben & Jerry's ahead of the spinout, as the renowned ice cream brand will make up a larger portion of the new company's sales.</p><p>Ben & Jerry's annual revenue of $1.28 billion accounts for almost 14% of Magnum's global turnover, compared to just 1.8% of Unilever.</p><p>The brand has been one of the few voices in corporate America speaking out against policies backed by U.S. President Donald Trump and Israel's war in Gaza.</p><p>Magnum warned that actions by Ben & Jerry's could result in reputational damage, boycotts and investor claims.</p><p>Meanwhile, the foundation makes donations to other non-profit organizations focused on issues ranging from racial equity to environmental protection.</p></body></title>",
            "summary": "<body><p>STORY: The Ben & Jerry\u2019s Foundation, a non-profit funded by the ice cream brand, was found to have deficiencies in financial controls and governance, as well as issues in conflicts of interest.</p><p>That\u2019s according to an audit by Magnum, the Unilever unit set to be spun off next week that will own Ben & Jerry\u2019s.</p><p>Magnum said it conducted an audit as a matter of good governance in preparation for the upcoming spin-off...</p><p>and added that they're trying to work with the foundation by adopting a code of ethics, conflict-of-interest policy, term limits for trustees and due diligence and financial controls on grants.</p><p>The foundation receives about $5 million annually from Ben & Jerry's, and Magnum said it plans to continue fully funding the organization, provided the issues are raised and addressed.</p><p>Ben & Jerry's and the foundation did not respond to requests for comment, but its co-founder Ben Cohen said in October that he expects the conflict between the brand and its new owner to grow after the spin-off.</p><p>Unilever and Magnum have been upping the pressure on Ben & Jerry's ahead of the spinout, as the renowned ice cream brand will make up a larger portion of the new company's sales.</p><p>Ben & Jerry's annual revenue of $1.28 billion accounts for almost 14% of Magnum's global turnover, compared to just 1.8% of Unilever.</p><p>The brand has been one of the few voices in corporate America speaking out against policies backed by U.S. President Donald Trump and Israel's war in Gaza.</p><p>Magnum warned that actions by Ben & Jerry's could result in reputational damage, boycotts and investor claims.</p><p>Meanwhile, the foundation makes donations to other non-profit organizations focused on issues ranging from racial equity to environmental protection.</p></body>",
